 "vo": [
  {
   "id": "v01",
   "t": 0.4,
   "chunks": [
    "New York's subway",
    "started as a turf war.",
    "Three rival systems,",
    "one city."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "v02",
   "t": 5.8,
   "chunks": [
    "In 1904, the IRT opened",
    "the first underground line.",
    "The city built it.",
    "A private company ran it."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "v03",
   "t": 13.8,
   "chunks": [
    "Then came the BMT,",
    "another private operator,",
    "spreading across",
    "Brooklyn and Queens."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "v04",
   "t": 20.3,
   "chunks": [
    "And in 1932,",
    "the city launched its own competitor:",
    "the IND."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "v05",
   "t": 25.0,
   "chunks": [
    "Three systems meant separate fares,",
    "duplicate routes,",
    "and lines that didn't connect.",
    "And a fixed nickel fare",
    "left the private companies struggling."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "v06",
   "t": 34.6,
   "chunks": [
    "So in 1940, the city bought out",
    "the IRT and BMT,",
    "and the three became ONE."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "v07",
   "t": 41.4,
   "chunks": [
    "Free transfers came later.",
    "And in 1968,",
    "the state's new MTA took over."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "v08",
   "t": 48.1,
   "chunks": [
    "Next up: the Interborough Express.",
    "A planned 14-mile light rail line",
    "linking Brooklyn and Queens,",
    "along an existing freight corridor."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "v09",
   "t": 58.0,
   "chunks": [
    "18 stations,",
    "and no detour through Manhattan."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "v10",
   "t": 61.7,
   "chunks": [
    "It's still in design",
    "and environmental review.",
    "No opening date yet."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "v11",
   "t": 66.9,
   "chunks": [
    "Congrats!",
    "You just rode through",
    "more than a century",
    "of subway history."
   ]
  }
 ]
}